We are interested in the dynamic case. We are given an instance of the necklace splitting 40
problem solved using an offline algorithm (see Section 2). We allow the following dynamic 41
updates: 42
Relocation BeadS[j1]is moved to index j2. 43
Insertionαkbeads of the same color are inserted into S. 44
Deletionαkbeads of the same color are deleted from S. 45
We can also allow for recoloring of beads by performing successive deletion and insertion. 46
1.2 Contributions 47
Here we give a brief overview of our contributions: 48
We introduce and formalize the problem of dynamic necklace splitting. 49
We design a linear-time algorithm for swapping adjacent beads when n= 2that achieves 50
the optimal bound of 2(k−1)cuts. We design two linear-time algorithms for relocation 51
of arbitrary distance when n= 2that achieve the optimal bound of 2(k−1)cuts. We 52
also design a linear-time algorithm for relocation with looser bounds for general n≥2 53
(Section 3). 54
We introduce the MinNodeMaxFlow problem and prove it to be NP-complete. We design 55
an approximation algorithm for special cases that we then use as part of an algorithm 56
to efficiently perform batch relocation when n= 2with the optimal number of cuts 57
(Section 4). 58
We design two linear-time algorithms for relocation with general n≥2whenm=nk 59
that achieve the optimal bound of n(k−1)cuts (Section 5). 60
We adapt our algorithm for batch relocation to be used for efficient insertion and deletion 61
(Section 6). 62
We design a randomized, polylogarithmic-time algorithm for relocation, insertion, and 63
deletion that produces an approximately fair set of cuts with high probability when n= 2 64
(Section 7). 65
See Table 2 for a comparison of our algorithms. 66
