The choice of which data structures to use to store information about the necklace has a 71
significant impact on the running time of our algorithms. We implement the necklace itself 72
as a doubly linked list, allowing for efficient dynamic updates. Each node/bead also stores 73
its index, the agent to whom it belongs, and a pointer to the next node/bead belonging to 74
the same agent. Given any bead, this allows us to find the subsequence of beads belonging 75
to the same agent in O(m/k)time. 76
Note that the cuts are stored implicitly and can be explicitly generated in O(m)time by 77
iterating through the necklace and identifying pairs of consecutive beads where the associated 78
agents switch. Alternatively, with minimal added cost, we can maintain a hash table mapping 79
each possible pair of agents to the set of cuts adjacent to both agents. 80
For certain algorithms, we need to make use of a neighborhood graph Gwhere each vertex 81
represents an agent and two vertices are joined by an edge if the corresponding agents possess 82
adjacent beads. We implement Gas an adjacency list. If the beads corresponding to k′83
agents are reassigned among the same agents, Gcan be updated in O(k′m/k)time. We 84
remove all edges incident to the k′agents and then determine which edges to add by iterating 85
through the k′m/kbeads assigned to those agents. 86
1.4 Related Work 87
Soon after the necklace splitting problem was originally introduced [ 6], Goldberg and West [ 9] 88
proved that a solution always exists if k= 2. Alon and West [ 3] gave a simpler proof using 89
the Borsuk–Ulam theorem [ 7], and Alon [ 1] generalized the results to k>2. Finally, Alon 90
and Graur [ 2] discovered an efficient approximation algorithm for finding a solution with few 91
cuts. Alon and Graur [ 2] also consider an online variation of the problem. They derive lower 92
and upper bounds on the number of cuts needed when k= 2based on the value of n. They 93
also generalize some of these results to the k>2case. 94
In addition to necklace splitting, other fair division problems can be extended to dynamic 95
settings. Kash et al. [ 13] study a fair division problem with divisible goods where agents with 96
Leontiefpreferencesarriveovertimeandgoodsmustbeirrevocablyallocated. Benadeetal.[ 5] 97
study the problem of allocating indivisible goods that arrive in an online manner, again with 98
irrevocable decisions. He et al. [ 11] generalize this to a setting where reallocation is allowed 99
but expensive. 100
