19:4 Dynamic Necklace Splitting
1.5 Applications 101
In this section, we discuss several key applications of the dynamic necklace splitting problem. 102
1.5.1 Fair Hash Maps 103
A core application of dynamic necklace splitting is designing practical fair hash maps. 104
In addition to ensuring fairness, such data-informed hash maps can even be faster than 105
traditional hash maps [ 14,17]. Shahbazi et al. [ 19] use the static necklace splitting problem 106
to design hash maps that satisfy group fairness. However, making dynamic updates to these 107
hash maps is infeasible without a dynamic solution to the necklace splitting problem. 108
For a concrete application, consider the use of fair hash maps to maintain user information 109
on a social network. Whenever new users join the network, their data needs to be efficiently 110
added to the hash map, and when users leave, their data needs to be efficiently removed. 111
As users’ attributes change, their data needs to be efficiently updated (corresponding to 112
relocation of beads in a necklace). 113
Another concrete application involves table joins in data lakes. Consider an organization 114
seeking to share its data with third parties. The data is stored in a data lake and includes 115
sensitive information as primary keys for certain tables. To protect user privacy, the data 116
needs to be hashed, which introduces the risk of hash collision. A fair hashing scheme 117
must be used to ensure any resulting errors when joining on the hashed columns do not 118
disproportionately impact a specific demographic group [ 19]. For this system to be efficient, 119
it needs to be easy to update the hashes as the data changes. 120
1.5.2 Load-Balancing 121
Consider the problem of load-balancing among multiple servers, where tasks need to be 122
completed in a specific order. Each agent corresponds to a server, and each bead color 123
corresponds to a different type of task. We want to spread the load across the servers evenly, 124
but we also want to minimize communication costs [ 12]. This necessitates having as few cuts 125
as possible. 126
If the order of tasks needs to be modified, new tasks need to be added, or existing tasks 127
need to be canceled, we would want to be able to update the computation plan without 128
recreating it from scratch. As a special case, if computation has already begun, we can 129
still perform dynamic updates that don’t affect the already-completed tasks using dynamic 130
necklace splitting. 131
1.5.3 Bucketization 132
Another key application is ensuring minority representation in bucketization. For example, 133
when partitioning data for machine learning tasks, it is crucial that the training data is 134
accurately represented by the test data for reliable results [ 18]. Intentionally designing the 135
bucketization process to maintain fairness helps prevent downstream fairness issues [ 15,16]. 136
Here, each agent represents a bucket, and the color of each bead represents the grouping 137
attribute. If the “red” data is more sparse than the “blue” data, it is important that the 138
relative proportions are preserved to have accurate results. 139

