2 Offline Algorithm 140
We present a simplified version of the algorithm of Shahbazi et al. [ 19] here, as it is used as 141
a subroutine in our algorithms for the n= 2case. Without loss of generality, we refer to 142
the colors as red and blue. Initialize a doubly linked list Lsuch that, for each j∈[0,m−1], 143
L[j]is the number of red beads in the substring [j,j+m/k−1]. Initialize a hash set H 144
that contains all indices jwhereL[j] =m1/k. This takes O(m)time. By the discrete 145
intermediate value theorem, there is a substring with m1/kred beads and m2/kblue beads, 146
soHis nonempty. We remove the smallest index jfromHand allocate the sublist of length 147
m/kbeginning at jinLto the first agent. We remove all indices within m/k−1beads ofj 148
inLfromH, update the values of Lform/k−1beads preceding j, add new indices to H 149
as necessary, and update the element preceding jinLto point to the next unallocated bead. 150
We repeat this process k−1more times to allocate the remaining beads. Each step takes 151
O(m/k)time, so in total, the algorithm takes O(m)time. When we use this algorithm as a 152
subroutine, we will often run it on a subsequence of length k′m/kwith onlyk′agents. For 153
this special case, the algorithm takes only O(k′m/k)time. 154
As shown by Alon and Graur [ 2], this algorithm produces at most 2(k−1)cuts. It is 155
also known that no algorithm can guarantee fewer than 2(k−1)cuts.2156
