3.2.1 The Path Algorithm 218
If the distance between A1andA2in the neighborhood graph Gis sufficiently small, we can 219
efficiently perform relocations without adding any extra cuts. First, we find a shortest path 220
betweenA1andA2inG. Letk′be the number of agents along that path. Then, we move 221
S[j1]to indexj2. Finally, we rerun the offline algorithm on the substring belonging to the k′222
agents. We will henceforth refer to this procedure as Path. 223
▶Theorem 4. Path produces a set of cuts of size at most 2(k−1)and has time complexity 224
O(k+k′m/k). 225
Proof.Note that the number of edges in Gis at most the number of cuts. Thus, finding 226
a shortest path takes O(k)time using breadth-first search, giving a running time of O(k+ 227
k′m/k). 228
