we will show that each update preserves that invariant, allowing us to use those properties in 231
the proof of the bound. 232
Each agent A′in the path shares a common boundary with the next agent in the path, 233
A′′, so there can be no agent whose allotted set of beads encloses that of A′but notA′′. 234
Without loss of generality, this enables us to conceptually alter the order of execution of 235
the original offline algorithm such that A′andA′′were assigned beads in consecutive turns. 236
Following the same logic, we can consider all of the k′agents to have had consecutive turns. 237
