3.2.2 The ColorPath Algorithm 276
Without loss of generality, assume S[j1]is red. We now construct a more intricate neighbor- 277
hood graph, G′. UnlikeG, this graph is directed and weighted. For each pair of agents A′278
andA′′, there is an edge from A′toA′′if the two agents possess adjacent beads. If any such 279
pair of beads has a red bead on the side of A′, we call the edge good, and its weight is 0; 280
otherwise, we call it bad, and its weight is 1. 281
First, we find a shortest path from A2toA1inG′. Letk′be the number of agents 282
along that path. Then, we move S[j1]to indexj2. Next, for each good edge, we move the 283
corresponding red bead across the adjacent cut. For each subpath of bad edges, we rerun the 284
offline algorithm on the substring corresponding to the subpath. We will henceforth refer to 285
this procedure as ColorPath . 286
Note that the number of edges in G′is at most twice the number of cuts. Finding a 287
shortest path takes O(k)time using Dial’s algorithm [ 8], giving a running time of O(k+k′m/k) 288
forColorPath . Asymptotically, this is the same as Path, but in practice, ColorPath will likely 289
be faster. 290
▶Proposition 7. ColorPath produces a set of cuts of size at most 2(k−1)and has time 291
complexity O(k+k′m/k). 292
3.2.3 The Fence Algorithm 293
If the number of agents k′in the path is large, we may instead wish to add extra cuts in 294
return for a reduced running time. After moving S[j1]to indexj2, instead of rerunning 295
the offline algorithm, we simply add (at most two) cuts around it as necessary. We will 296
henceforth refer to this procedure as Fence. 297
The main disadvantage of Fenceis that the resulting allocation is not guaranteed to be 298
one that could have been generated by an execution of the offline algorithm. This means that, 299

