and color that points to the corresponding bead in S. 437
5.1 Adjacent Indices 438
Suppose we need to swap a red bead (on the left) and blue bead (on the right) between A1439
andA2, respectively. There are three possible cases based on whether or not there is a cut 440
to the left of the red bead and/or the right of the blue bead (Table 3). 441
Table 3The possible cases for the DenseSwap algorithm.
Case Necklace
1RB→BR
2RBG→BRG
3aBRB→BBR→BBR
3bGRB→GBR→GBR
In the first case, there is a cut to the left of the red bead and to the right of the blue 442
bead (or it is at the end of the necklace). This is the simplest case – we swap the beads and 443
let the agents retain ownership of their original beads. No cuts need to be adjusted. 444
In the second case, there is a cut on the left, but not the right. We swap the beads and do 445
not adjust any cuts. A2takes possession of the left interval, and A1takes possession of the 446
right interval. As part of the right interval, A1gains at least one unneeded extra bead (the 447
green one). Each extra bead lacks a cut to its left, so they must be the first of their colors. 448
Thus, the original beads of A1are somewhere to the right in the necklace. Furthermore, 449
since each of the original beads is not the first of its color, they each start their own intervals. 450
A2takes ownership of each of these intervals and repeats the process with each of them if 451
they have multiple beads, continuing to attempt to re-satisfy the fairness constraint. Since 452
the necklace has exactly n−1pairs of adjacent beads without a cut, this results in at most 453
nownership exchanges throughout the procedure. 454
In the third case, there is no cut on the left. We swap the beads and move the cut left 455
by one bead (to ensure that the set of cuts maintains the structure of one generated by the 456
offline algorithm). A1andA2retain the left and right intervals, respectively. Similarly to 457
the second case, to maintain the fairness constraint, at most n−1intervals will have to be 458
exchanged between the two agents. 459
We will henceforth refer to this procedure as DenseSwap . The two-dimensional array 460
allows us to perform each ownership exchange in constant time, giving a running time of 461
O(n)forDenseSwap . 462
▶Theorem 15. DenseSwap produces a set of cuts of size exactly n(k−1)and has time 463
complexity O(n). 464
5.2 Nonadjacent Indices 465
We can generalize the ideas from the previous section to design an algorithm for arbitrary 466
relocation. In addition to the two-dimensional array, we maintain for each color a red–black 467
ICDT 2026

