tree tracking the indices of the beads of that color in S. Suppose we need to relocate a red 468
bead. There are four possible cases. 469
In the first case, the bead to be relocated begins as the first of its color and remains 470
so. We move the bead to the desired position, and if the interval is owned by a different 471
agent, we re-satisfy the fairness constraint by exchanging at most nintervals between the 472
two agents, as in the previous section. 473
In the second case, the bead to be relocated begins not as the first of its color and remains 474
so. We move the bead and its corresponding cut to an arbitrary boundary to make it a 475
singleton interval, and if the two affected intervals are not owned by the same agent, we 476
re-satisfy the fairness constraint by exchanging at most n−1intervals between the two 477
agents. Then, we move the bead and its corresponding cut to the desired position, and if 478
the destination interval does not belong to the owner of the bead, we re-satisfy the fairness 479
constraint by exchanging at most nintervals between the two agents. 480
In the third case, the bead begins as the first of its color but does not remain so. We 481
relocate the bead directly to the left of the second red bead via Case 1, and then we relocate 482
the second red bead to the desired position via Case 2. At most 3n−1intervals are exchanged 483
throughout this case. 484
In the fourth case, the bead begins not as the first of its color but becomes the first. We 485
relocate the bead and its corresponding cut directly to the right of the first red bead via 486
Case 2, and then we relocate the first red bead to the desired position via Case 1. At most 487
3n−1intervals are exchanged throughout this case. 488
We will henceforth refer to this procedure as DenseJump . Updating the red–black tree in 489
Case 2 takes O(k)time if the rank of the bead changes. Otherwise, all updates to the tree 490
take constant time. This gives us a total running time of O(k+n)forDenseJump . 491
▶Theorem 16. DenseJump produces a set of cuts of size exactly n(k−1)and has time 492
complexity O(k+n). 493
