▶Proposition 20. For everyk, there exists a necklace that requires exactly 2(k−1)cuts. 624
Proof.Thek= 1case is trivial, so we assume k >1. Consider the necklace withm
2red 625
beads followed bym
2blue beads. Suppose we have an optimal set of cuts of the necklace. 626
The first interval in the resulting allocation consists of at mostm
2kred beads. Without loss 627
of generality, assume this interval belongs to A1. If it has exactlym
2kbeads, we move to 628
the next interval (without loss of generality, owned by A2). Else, we check if the first two 629
intervals combined have more thanm
2kbeads. If so, we can move the first cut to the right 630
until the first interval has exactlym
2kbeads. To maintain the fairness constraint, we reassign 631
all the red beads of A1in the rest of the necklace to A2(without adding any cuts). We then 632
move to the next interval. Instead, if the first two intervals combined have at mostm
2kbeads, 633
we remove the cut between them and add one cut within another interval with red beads 634
belonging to A1, maintaining the overall number of cuts. 635
We repeat this process from both the left (red) and right (blue) ends of the necklace 636
until we reach the center interval, which consists ofm
2kred beads andm
2kblue beads. We 637
have exactly 2(k−1)cuts, and since the number of cuts remained constant throughout this 638
procedure, the original optimal set of cuts was of size 2(k−1). ◀ 639
B NP-completeness of MinNodeMaxFlow 640
Proof of Proposition 9. First, we show that MinNodeMaxFlow is in NP. We are given a flow 641
network and a limit κon the number of active nodes as input and a proposed flow as a 642
certificate. We can compute the value of a max flow on the graph (in polynomial time). 643
Then we can easily verify whether the proposed flow has that value and satisfies the flow 644
constraints and whether the number of active nodes is at most κ.MinNodeMaxFlow is in NP. 645
Next, we show that MinNodeMaxFlow is NP-hard, via reduction from Vertex Cover . Given 646
an undirected graph Γ = (VΓ,EΓ)and parameter κ, we want to determine whether Γhas 647
a vertex cover of size at most κ. We construct an auxiliary, directed graph Γ′= (VΓ′,EΓ′) 648
containing the same vertices in addition to a source node σ, a sink node τ, and an extra node 649
uvfor every edge (u,v)∈EΓ. We replace each edge (u,v)∈EΓwith two infinite-capacity 650
edges: (uv,u )and(uv,v). We add a unit-capacity edge from σto each extra node uvand 651
an infinite-capacity edge from each original node utoτ. 652
If there is a vertex cover of size κinΓ, then there is a max flow on Γ′with|EΓ|+κ 653
active nodes. Conversely, suppose there is a max flow on Γ′withκactive nodes. If any 654
nodeuvis sending fractional flow to each of its neighbors, we can reroute its flow to go to 655
just one neighbor without changing the number of active nodes. We would need to do this 656
at most|EΓ|times, so we can always efficiently find an integral solution, given the initial 657
solution. Once we obtain an integral solution, we have a corresponding vertex cover of size κ. 658
Therefore, MinNodeMaxFlow is NP-hard. ◀ 659

